The code statements that lead to the bug are:
while (dum->callback_usage > 0) {
    spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dum->lock, flags);
    usleep_range(1000, 2000);
    spin_lock_irqsave(&dum->lock, flags);
}
Reason:
This synchronization loop in dummy_pullup() was executed before emulated interrupts were disabled (dum->ints_enabled still true). As a result, new interrupt callbacks could be scheduled after the loop completed, allowing callback handlers to run after the gadget driver had been unbound, causing a race condition.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!enable) {
    while (dum->callback_usage > 0) {
        spin_unlock_irq(&dum->lock);
        usleep_range(1000, 2000);
        spin_lock_irq(&dum->lock);
    }
}
Reason:
The synchronization loop is moved to dummy_udc_async_callbacks() and placed after setting dum->ints_enabled = false. This ensures that all pending callback handlers finish before any new interrupts can be emulated, and it occurs before the unbind callback—correctly emulating synchronize_irq() as in gadget/udc/core. The old loop was removed from dummy_pullup().

